nt-strategy-2026-27.pdf","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","date":"25/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"Statement of Accounts 2025/26 Preparation and Accounting Policies Update","url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s136178/statement-of-accounts-2025-26-preparation-and-accounting-pol.pdf","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","date":"25/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"Treasury Management half year outturn report 2025-26 County Council","url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s136092/treasury-management-half-year-outturn-report-2025-26-county.pdf","committee":"County Council","date":"19/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"Appendix N - Treasury Management Strategy 2026-27","url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s134623/appendix-n-treasury-management-strategy-2026-27.pdf","committee":"County Council","date":"12/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"Appendix P - Capital Strategy","url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s134625/appendix-p-capital-strategy.pdf","committee":"County Council","date":"12/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"Appendix Q - Minimum Revenue Provision statement","url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s134626/appendix-q-minimum-revenue-provision-statement.pdf","committee":"County Council","date":"12/02/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"Health Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"County Council","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Growth, Environment & Transport Cabinet Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Children, Young People and Education Cabinet Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"County Council","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Policy and Resources Cabinet Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Adult Social Care and Public Health Cabinet Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Adult Social Care Older People services overspent by \u00a38.9m; residential care unit costs and community support usage above budget.","lead":"Adult social care is a large cost base (\u00a3786.7m budget, 47% of General Fund) with inelastic demand. Rising unit costs and usage pressures here indicate demographic and social care market pressures that are structural and likely to recur.","iso":"2026-10-01","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Adult Social Care and Children's Services forecast to deliver 86-90% of savings targets; strategic service changes slipping.","lead":"Large service directorates are not delivering planned savings at the expected pace. Commissioning and strategic transformation milestones (contract mobilizations, new service models, facility openings) are slipping, indicating either over-ambitious targets\u2026","iso":"2026-10-01","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Early debt redemption of \u00a360m in 2025-26 generated \u00a30.683m discount and \u00a32.420m annual interest saving.","lead":"Early redemption reflects active debt management and sound rate-risk positioning. However, it reduced liquidity headroom and necessitated compensating short-term borrowing, indicating the council is managing cash flow tightly without significant buffer.","iso":"2026-10-01","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care Older People services overspent by \u00a38.9m; residential care unit costs and community support usage above budget.","detail":"Adult Social Care & Health directorate shows a net overspend of \u00a39.1m. The primary driver is Older People Residential Care Services at +\u00a36.4m, caused by average placement costs being higher than budget and lower-than-expected income from service user contributions (reflecting lower numbers paying full cost of care). A secondary pressure is Older People Community Based Services at +\u00a32.1m, driven by higher Direct Payments volumes and lower income from client charges. These service lines sit within a long-term support division budgeted at \u00a3696.6m.","category":"overspend","matters":"Adult social care is a large cost base (\u00a3786.7m budget, 47% of General Fund) with inelastic demand. Rising unit costs and usage pressures here indicate demographic and social care market pressures that are structural and likely to recur.","figures":"Adult Social Care & Health total budget \u00a3786.7m, forecast \u00a3795.8m (overspend \u00a39.1m or 1.2%). Older People residential care overspend \u00a36.4m, community services overspend \u00a32.1m.","quote":"Pressures on this service line relate to the average costs of placements being higher than budgeted for which has been impacted by undeliverable savings, alongside reduced levels of income which is due to a combination of factors including reductions in the number of service users who pay for the full cost of their care.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care and Children's Services forecast to deliver 86-90% of savings targets; strategic service changes slipping.","detail":"Adult Social Care forecast to deliver \u00a337.3m of \u00a341.7m savings (89.6%), with \u00a32.0m shortfall due to delayed implementation of a new Older People Residential & Nursing contract planned for Q2/3 rather than Q1. Children, Young People & Education forecast to deliver \u00a318.4m of \u00a321.3m (86.4%), with \u00a30.953m of an invest-to-save in-house residential scheme not landing due to delayed home openings, \u00a30.704m of residential special schools savings unachieved, and \u00a30.601m of legal services efficiencies depending on Court proceedings acceleration.","category":"savings","matters":"Large service directorates are not delivering planned savings at the expected pace. Commissioning and strategic transformation milestones (contract mobilizations, new service models, facility openings) are slipping, indicating either over-ambitious targets or constrained delivery capability.","figures":"Adult Social Care: \u00a341.7m target, \u00a337.3m forecast (89.6%), \u00a32.0m shortfall. Children, Young People & Education: \u00a321.3m target, \u00a318.4m forecast (86.4%), principal shortfalls \u00a30.953m residential units, \u00a30.704m schools, \u00a30.601m legal.","quote":"A new Older People Residential & Nursing (OPRN) contract is planned for Quarter 2/3 which will introduce a new sustainable pricing model. Ahead of implementation ASC are managing cost pressures during the transition period ensuring affordability of all new placements until the new contract is mobilised.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","headline":"Early debt redemption of \u00a360m in 2025-26 generated \u00a30.683m discount and \u00a32.420m annual interest saving.","detail":"The council repaid \u00a350m of debt in September 2025 and \u00a310m in March 2025 ahead of maturity, obtaining a 10-year discount of \u00a30.683m and achieving an annual interest cost saving of \u00a32.420m. This was deliberate treasury strategy to lock in savings at favourable relative rates. However, the report notes that this action reduced cash balances and created short-term borrowing requirements, indicating tight liquidity management.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Early redemption reflects active debt management and sound rate-risk positioning. However, it reduced liquidity headroom and necessitated compensating short-term borrowing, indicating the council is managing cash flow tightly without significant buffer.","figures":"Debt redemptions \u00a350m (September 2025) and \u00a310m (March 2025), discount \u00a30.683m, annual interest saving \u00a32.420m.","quote":"Annual discount received for 10 years on \u00a350m early debt redemption in September 2025 and \u00a310m in March 2025 ... Impact on debt interest costs of \u00a350m early debt redemption in 2025-26","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","headline":"General Fund reserves drawn down by \u00a321.4m to fund in-year overspend; will fall to minimum threshold of 5.1%.","detail":"The \u00a321.4m revenue overspend for 2026-27 must be funded from reserves. General Fund reserves are forecast to move from -\u00a366.0m to -\u00a384.8m, representing 5.1% of the net revenue budget (\u00a31,655.6m). This comprises: repayment of previous overspend drawdown (\u00a320.2m), rebuilding contribution (\u00a316.8m), and current year overspend drawdown (\u00a321.4m). The council's stated minimum target is 5% of net revenue budget.","category":"reserves","matters":"Drawing down reserves to fund operational overspend weakens financial resilience and increases future replenishment requirements. At the minimum threshold with no buffer, any further adverse variance will cause the reserve to fall below policy minimum.","figures":"General Fund reserve opening balance -\u00a366.0m, forecast closing -\u00a384.8m (5.1% of \u00a31,655.6m net revenue budget), in-year overspend funded from reserves \u00a321.4m.","quote":"When the council overspends, it must fund that overspend from reserves. Any overspend is a concern for the authority and presents a risk to the Council's future financial sustainability and it is essential that the need to drawdown from reserves is reduced as far as possible, as drawdowns from reserves weaken the Council's financial resilience and increase the requirement to replenish reserves in future years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"26-00055 - Revenue and Capital Budget Forecast Outturn Report \u2013 Quarter 1, 2026-2027","paper_url":"https://democracy.kent.gov.uk/documents/s139790/139790.pdf","headline":"\u00a34.1m of planned savings for 2026-27 are undeliverable; \u00a35.1m delayed to future years.","detail":"Of the \u00a391.7m gross savings target for 2026-27, the council forecasts delivery of only \u00a382.5m (90%). Of the \u00a39.2m shortfall, \u00a34.1m is identified as undeliverable (will not be achieved). Key undeliverable items: \u00a32.0m Adult Social Care commissioning (new contract mobilization delayed to Q2/3), \u00a30.7m legal savings (dependent on Courts speeding up proceedings, deemed unlikely), \u00a30.6m contingent on Government actions on residential care costs. A further \u00a35.1m of planned savings will now be delivered in future years instead.","category":"savings","matters":"Undeliverable savings indicate structural overspend: the underlying cost base is higher than planned. Savings targets of this scale not materializing suggest either the plans were over-ambitious or delivery capability is constrained. This implies future-year budgets face similar slippage risk.","figures":"Savings target \u00a391.7m, forecast delivery \u00a382.5m (90%), undeliverable \u00a34.1m, delayed to future years \u00a35.1m.","quote":"As at Quarter 1, \u00a382.5m is expected to be delivered in 2026-27, which represents 90% delivery against the target. \u00a39.2m of savings are currently not expected to be delivered in 2026-27. Of this amount, \u00a35.1m is planned to be delivered in future financial years, with the remaining \u00a34.1m no longer deliverable.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-08-03"},"name":"Kent CC","findings_total":78,"news_drafts_total":3}}